{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Though the fig tree</b> He mentioned everything that produces blood and by which a person can live. And he mentioned figs, because the moist and even the dry ones generate and produce in a person much blood by which he lives, and likewise grapes and the fruit of the tree, like nut and almond and pomegranate and apple, do not produce blood." "And the word <b>produce</b> is like \"and the land shall give her produce\" (Joel 2:22). And olive oil, when a person cooks vegetables in it, also generates blood." "And the meaning of <b>fail</b> is as though there were no olive in its growth." "<b>And the fields</b> is like \"and a field before it arose\" (Micah 2:4). He also mentioned flesh." "<b>Flock</b> is cut off — like \"from your folds\" (1 Kings 5:3)." "<b>Stalls</b> are the place of their lodging."], :book :habakkuk, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {4 "Ibn Ezra's lemma here is a single word from the verse; the comparison is to a word sharing the same root meaning fold/pen."}, :source-witnesses [["<b>Ibn Ezra on Habakkuk</b>- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/havakuk"] ["Ibn Ezra on Habakkuk -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/havakuk"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:48:59.520504157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}